research/audit/wave8-preserved-refuters/ex-cauchy-product-of-geometric-series--ccab5a4d1f551c58.prompt.md-738-**Given:** Sequences $(a_k)$ and $(b_k)$ with $\sum |a_k|$ and $\sum |b_k|$ convergent, sums $L_a$ and $L_b$ respectively, partial sums $P_N = \sum_{k<N}|a_k|$ and $Q_m = \sum_{j<m}|b_j|$, and the Cauchy product $c_n = \sum_{k=0}^{n} a_k b_{n-k}$ ([[def-cauchy-product]]).
research/audit/wave8-preserved-refuters/ex-cauchy-product-of-geometric-series--ccab5a4d1f551c58.prompt.md-739-
research/audit/wave8-preserved-refuters/ex-cauchy-product-of-geometric-series--ccab5a4d1f551c58.prompt.md:740:[L1] The finite identity of [[thm-mertens]], claim 1: for arbitrary sequences $(x_k)$, $(y_k)$ with partial sums $Y_m = \sum_{j<m} y_j$ and Cauchy product $(z_n)$, one has $\sum_{n<N} z_n = \sum_{i<N} x_i\, Y_{N-i}$ for every $N$.
research/audit/wave8-preserved-refuters/ex-cauchy-product-of-geometric-series--ccab5a4d1f551c58.prompt.md-741-
research/audit/wave8-preserved-refuters/ex-cauchy-product-of-geometric-series--ccab5a4d1f551c58.prompt.md:742:[L2] Mertens' theorem, claim 2 of [[thm-mertens]]: if $\sum x_k$ converges absolutely and $\sum y_k$ converges, their Cauchy product converges to the product of the sums.
