research/audit/wave8-preserved-refuters/ex-cauchy-product-of-geometric-series--ccab5a4d1f551c58.prompt.md-776-## Remarks
research/audit/wave8-preserved-refuters/ex-cauchy-product-of-geometric-series--ccab5a4d1f551c58.prompt.md-777-
research/audit/wave8-preserved-refuters/ex-cauchy-product-of-geometric-series--ccab5a4d1f551c58.prompt.md:778:- **Only claim 1 of [[thm-mertens]] is reused, and it is reused for a different pair of sequences.** The identity there is proved for arbitrary real sequences and carries no convergence hypothesis, which is exactly what allows it to be applied here to $(|a_k|)$ and $(|b_k|)$. Nothing about the absolute values is reproved.
research/audit/wave8-preserved-refuters/ex-cauchy-product-of-geometric-series--ccab5a4d1f551c58.prompt.md-779-
research/audit/wave8-preserved-refuters/ex-cauchy-product-of-geometric-series--ccab5a4d1f551c58.prompt.md-780-- **The bound is not an equality.** The sum of $\sum|c_n|$ can be strictly less than $L_a L_b$, since cancellation inside each $c_n$ is invisible to $\gamma_n$; the inequality is all that is claimed and all that is needed.
