research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-cauchy-product-of-geometric-series.md-660-## Facts & Assumptions
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-cauchy-product-of-geometric-series.md-661-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-cauchy-product-of-geometric-series.md-662-**Given:** Sequences $(a_k)$ and $(b_k)$ with $\sum |a_k|$ and $\sum |b_k|$ convergent, sums $L_a$ and $L_b$ respectively, partial sums $P_N = \sum_{k<N}|a_k|$ and $Q_m = \sum_{j<m}|b_j|$, and the Cauchy product $c_n = \sum_{k=0}^{n} a_k b_{n-k}$ ([[def-cauchy-product]]).
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-cauchy-product-of-geometric-series.md-663-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-cauchy-product-of-geometric-series.md:664:[L1] The finite identity of [[thm-mertens]], claim 1: for arbitrary sequences $(x_k)$, $(y_k)$ with partial sums $Y_m = \sum_{j<m} y_j$ and Cauchy product $(z_n)$, one has $\sum_{n<N} z_n = \sum_{i<N} x_i\, Y_{N-i}$ for every $N$.
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-cauchy-product-of-geometric-series.md-665-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-cauchy-product-of-geometric-series.md:666:[L2] Mertens' theorem, claim 2 of [[thm-mertens]]: if $\sum x_k$ converges absolutely and $\sum y_k$ converges, their Cauchy product converges to the product of the sums.
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-cauchy-product-of-geometric-series.md-667-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-cauchy-product-of-geometric-series.md-668-[L3] $\bigl|\sum_{k<n} x_k\bigr| \le \sum_{k<n}|x_k|$ ([[lem-triangle-inequality-finite]]).
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-cauchy-product-of-geometric-series.md-669-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-cauchy-product-of-geometric-series.md-670-[L4] Absolute value: $|xy| = |x|\,|y|$ and $|x| \ge 0$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]]).
--
