research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-retry1-thm-mertens.prompt.md-1391-
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-retry1-thm-mertens.prompt.md-1392-- **The lower bound is not merely nonzero: it grows to $2$.** Step 4.1 gives $|c_n| \ge 2\iota(n+1)/\iota(n+2) = 2 - 2/\iota(n+2)$, and that bound increases to $2$; so the terms of the Cauchy product do not shrink at all, and the divergence is detected by the crudest test available. What the size of $|c_n|$ itself tends to is not determined here and is not needed.
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-retry1-thm-mertens.prompt.md-1393-
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-retry1-thm-mertens.prompt.md:1394:- **Where the failure comes from.** In $c_n$ every one of the $n+1$ products $a_k b_{n-k}$ carries the same sign $\varepsilon_n$, so no cancellation occurs *within* $c_n$: the alternation that makes each factor converge is exactly what aligns the terms of the product. Absolute convergence of one factor, as in [[thm-mertens]], prevents this by making the total mass finite.
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-retry1-thm-mertens.prompt.md-1395-
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-retry1-thm-mertens.prompt.md-1396-- **The claim becomes true under other hypotheses.** If all three series $\sum a_k$, $\sum b_k$ and $\sum c_n$ are assumed to converge, then the sum of the product is the product of the sums; but that theorem is proved through power series and Abel's limit theorem, which are later in the reading order. The companion examples page records the same witness from the other side.
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-retry1-thm-mertens.prompt.md-1397-````
